The Security Council,

Recalling all its previous resolutions and statements of its President on the situation in Somalia,

Reaffirming its respect for the sovereignty, territorial integrity, political independence and unity of Somalia, and its commitment to the peaceful resolution of disputes in the region,

Determining that acts of terrorism, including those by Al-Shabaab, and the activities of Islamic State in Somalia continue to constitute a threat to international peace and security,

Condemning in the strongest terms the continued attacks by Al-Shabaab against civilians, the Federal Government of Somalia, the Somali National Army, the African Union Support and Stabilization Mission in Somalia (AUSSOM), and international partners, including recent attacks in Middle Shabelle and Mogadishu, and expressing deep concern at the loss of life and the humanitarian impact of these attacks,

Expressing concern at the threat posed by the proliferation of improvised explosive device (IED) components, illicit arms trafficking, the illegal charcoal trade and piracy, and stressing the need for enhanced regional and international cooperation to address these threats,

Welcoming the deployment of AUSSOM and the progress made in Somalia’s security transition, commending the contributions of troop- and police-contributing countries, and stressing the importance of coordinated international support to Somali-led security efforts,

Expressing grave concern at the humanitarian situation in Somalia, including the impact of terrorism and armed conflict on civilians, and reaffirming the need for safe, unhindered and sustainable humanitarian access while respecting humanitarian principles,

Reaffirming the importance of sanctions being targeted and proportionate, with clear and effective humanitarian exemptions, and recalling the need for fair and clear procedures for listing and delisting,

Recalling the mandate of the Panel of Experts on Somalia and the Security Council Committee pursuant to resolution 751 (1992) concerning Somalia,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Decides to renew until 28 February 2026 the measures imposed by resolution 1844 (2008), as renewed and modified by subsequent resolutions, including the provisions set out in paragraphs 15 and 17 of resolution 2182 (2014), as expanded by paragraph 5 of resolution 2607 (2021) to cover improvised explosive device components, as most recently renewed by paragraph 1 of resolution 2775 (2025), and recalls that these measures include the arms embargo, travel ban, asset freeze and charcoal ban;

2. Reaffirms that the measures set out in paragraph 1 above do not apply to the timely provision of humanitarian assistance, including medical assistance, food, shelter and other items necessary for the basic needs of civilians, in accordance with the humanitarian exemption procedures of the Committee, and requests the Committee to expedite the consideration of exemption requests and to make relevant information publicly available;

3. Decides to extend the mandate of the Panel of Experts established pursuant to resolution 751 (1992) until 28 February 2026, and requests the Panel to submit to the Security Council, through the Committee, a midterm report by 15 October 2025 and a final report by 15 January 2026, and to provide regular updates to the Committee;

4. Calls upon all Member States, in particular the Federal Government of Somalia, the Federal Member States and regional States, to comply fully with the measures set out in paragraph 1 above, to cooperate fully with the Committee and the Panel of Experts, and to provide timely and accurate information on violations, including with respect to IED components, illicit arms trafficking and the illegal charcoal trade;

5. Urges all Member States, regional organizations and international partners to strengthen cooperation, including information-sharing, to prevent the supply, sale or transfer of weapons, ammunition and IED components to Al-Shabaab and other armed groups, while respecting international law;

6. Calls for enhanced coordination between the United Nations, the African Union, AUSSOM, the Federal Government of Somalia and international partners to degrade Al-Shabaab and Islamic State in Somalia, including through support for Somali-led security efforts and strengthened weapons and ammunition management;

7. Decides to review the measures set out in paragraph 1 above and the mandate of the Panel of Experts by 28 February 2026, with a view to considering further adjustments, and expresses its intention to keep the situation under active review;

8. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

